when scientists assess new variants  before deeming them variants of concern  they look to see whether the new  mutations are able to do three things  one make the virus resistant to the  effect of the vaccines  two make the virus more transmissible  when compared to existing variants  three  make people more sick if they were to  contract the new variant  the omicron variant has more mutations  than any previous variants many of which  are on the spike protein the part of the  virus it uses to bind to and infect  human cells  the most widely used vaccines pfizer  moderna oxford astrazeneca are based on  getting our immune systems to recognize  the spy protein part of the coronavirus  as foreign if the mutations in the  omicron variant change the sky protein  enough so that our immune systems can no  longer fully recognize it then it may  mean there is a degree of immune escape  for this variant but the chances of it  rendering previous protection useless is  extremely low and it is far more likely  the vaccines will continue to offer a  good degree of protection from even this  variant  recent surges in new infections in south  africa where the omicron variant has  been identified has raised serious  concerns over increased transmissibility  of this new variant particularly in  younger people  although still too early to know for  sure it looks as though the mutations  omicron has may make it more  transmissible when compared to delta  there is currently no data to support  that those that are infected with  omicron suffer any different symptoms to  those who caught and were infected with  previous variants and there are some  reports that it may actually result in a  milder illness than delta though we need  more data on this  there are a lot of unknowns at play  right now but scientists are working at  record speed to get a better  understanding of the omicron variant and  what it might mean for the pandemic  precautions taken now will mean we can  keep things under control until we  understand more about this variant
 

